      "id": 1,
      "text": "Atwood was originally called Mount Ruska and was laid out by Harvey Hunt and Agnes Teegarden on September 29, 1857.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atwood%2C_Indiana"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town's name changed to Atwood in 1865 after a citizen petition, and a post office opened in 1864.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26,
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atwood%2C_Indiana"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Atwood grew around the Pittsburgh, Fort Wayne, and Chicago Railway that crossed the area in 1856, making it a key trading point.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atwood%2C_Indiana"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The first Methodist church was built in 1868 on land donated by Ira Atwood, using lumber from nearby Carpenter’s Woods.",
